# Vaultwarden ops

## Gate

Use when the user asks about Vaultwarden / Bitwarden-compatible **admin
ops**: instance health, user/org inventory, invites, SMTP/config shape
(non-secret), or admin-token gated diagnostics. Skip HashiCorp Vault
asks. Skip “show me the password for …” — that is refuse-leak, not ops.

## Transport

No Vaultwarden MCP was discovered for this harness. Prefer:

1. Vaultwarden **Admin** API / admin UI with a resolved base URL and
   admin token from the operator’s secret store / env.
2. Else Bitwarden-compatible API for **org/collection/user metadata
   only** — never for cipher plaintext.

If a Vaultwarden MCP appears later, use its admin inventory tools when
ready — **do not invent MCP tool names**. Never hardcode hosts or paste
admin tokens into chat.

Resolve base URL from operator config / env (for example
`VAULTWARDEN_URL`) — never invent an internal hostname.

Admin auth shape (token value never echoed):

```text
Authorization: Bearer <admin-token>
# or the instance’s documented admin-token header / cookie flow
```

Confirm the exact admin routes for the running version via the
instance’s admin docs or OpenAPI if exposed — path prefixes vary
(` /admin `, `/api/...`). Prefer list/get that return **counts and
statuses**, not cipher bodies.

## Resolve ids

Never invent user id, org id, or collection id. Resolve from the user,
a prior list this turn, or admin list endpoints. If several match, list
candidates (email/name + status) and ask once.

## Receipt shape

Report: **user email/name**, **org name**, **status** (enabled /
verified / invites pending), **counts**. Never report:

- login passwords, secure notes, card numbers, identities
- TOTP seeds, passkeys, attachment bytes
- cipher JSON/CSV exports
- RSA data keys, `data.json`, admin tokens

Always load `vaultwarden-refuse-leak` with this rule.

## Admin inventory (read)

Typical admin needs (adjust paths to the instance version after checking
docs — do not invent MCP tools):

| Need | Approach |
| --- | --- |
| Users | Admin users list — id, email, created, enabled |
| Orgs | Admin orgs list — name, user count, collections count |
| Invites | Pending invites metadata (email + org) — no passwords |
| Diagnostics | Version / config **flags** (SMTP configured? Y/N) — not SMTP passwords |

Cap listings; summarize large user tables.

## Config shape (non-secret)

When asked how the instance is configured, report **booleans and names
only**: signup allowed, invitations, SSO/OIDC enabled, attachment limit
class, domain setting **hostname form** the operator already named. Do
not dump env files, `ADMIN_TOKEN`, database URLs, or SMTP credentials.

## Mutate (gated)

User delete, org delete, invite revoke, disable user, wipe collections
→ `vaultwarden-safety` plus an explicit verb naming the object. Prefer
disable over delete when the user did not say delete.

## Defaults

1. Read-only admin inventory is the default.
2. Prefer the user’s own Bitwarden/Vaultwarden client for reading any
   secret material.
3. HashiCorp Vault and other secret stores are out of scope here.
4. After an authorized mutate, re-list and report status — still zero
   cipher bytes.

## Refuse

- Inventing user/org/collection ids.
- Any cipher plaintext, export, or “debug by pasting the vault.”
- Pasting admin tokens or DB URLs into chat.
- Claiming a user/org exists without a list this turn.
